1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,215
Remember Asha's biscuit jar game at the
Corner Cafe?

2
00:00:04,235 --> 00:00:07,050
A player guesses 7, then 12, then 15.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:12,400
Each guess lives in one variable, so each
new guess replaces the one before.

4
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:15,661
At the end, the game cannot show a single
guess.

5
00:00:15,681 --> 00:00:21,141
Asha's day report from Chapter 9 had the
same problem: it added up the sales, but

6
00:00:21,161 --> 00:00:22,900
could not show them again.

7
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,651
This is Code Quest, Chapter 11.

8
00:00:25,671 --> 00:00:30,440
Today you learn lists: one variable that
keeps many values, in order.

9
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:34,991
And an AI assistant will promise the three
biggest sales of the day.

10
00:00:35,011 --> 00:00:36,795
Will its code deliver them?

11
00:00:36,815 --> 00:00:37,798
You will check.

12
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:40,395
So here is today's mission.

13
00:00:40,415 --> 00:00:44,680
Asha's day report keeps every sale in one
list, on line 2.

14
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:49,861
Then it reports the total, the average,
the biggest and the smallest sale, and the

15
00:00:49,881 --> 00:00:50,430
best two.

16
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:53,965
And it still shows the sales in the order
they happened.

17
00:00:53,985 --> 00:00:54,908
Let's build it.

18
00:00:55,483 --> 00:00:58,953
Read any item of a list, and add or remove
items.

19
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,550
Take a part with a slice, and build a list
in a loop.

20
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:04,626
And sort a list, and add it up.

21
00:01:05,217 --> 00:01:09,710
A list keeps many values in one variable,
in a fixed order.

22
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:14,377
And a list of 3 sales needs the same code
as a list of 3,000.

23
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,379
Your music playlist is a list.

24
00:01:16,399 --> 00:01:20,045
So is your shopping basket, and the
messages in a chat.

25
00:01:20,617 --> 00:01:26,043
A list is written with square brackets,
and commas between the items.

26
00:01:26,063 --> 00:01:28,017
This list has four items.

27
00:01:28,067 --> 00:01:30,665
Each item has an index: its position.

28
00:01:30,685 --> 00:01:32,151
Counting starts at 0.

29
00:01:32,171 --> 00:01:35,407
So tea is at index 0, and scone is at
index 3.

30
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:37,818
Negative indexes count from the end.

31
00:01:37,838 --> 00:01:42,160
Minus 1 is always the last item, whatever
the length of the list.

32
00:01:42,753 --> 00:01:48,643
In Chapter 3, you learned that a string
cannot be changed after it is made.

33
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:49,345
A list can.

34
00:01:49,365 --> 00:01:54,167
You can replace an item, add one or remove
one, and the list itself changes.

35
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:55,942
We say a list is mutable.

36
00:01:55,962 --> 00:01:58,126
It can change after it is made.

37
00:01:58,720 --> 00:01:59,449
Quick check.

38
00:01:59,469 --> 00:02:02,071
Which one can you change after it is made?

39
00:02:13,447 --> 00:02:13,575
C.

40
00:02:13,595 --> 00:02:14,095
A list.

41
00:02:14,115 --> 00:02:16,101
A string cannot be changed.

42
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:19,221
Its methods give you a new string instead.

43
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:20,860
Take a breather.

44
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:22,780
Here is what you know so far.

45
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,219
Next, Asha writes her first list.

46
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:30,434
Step 1.

47
00:02:30,454 --> 00:02:36,054
Asha writes today's first order as a list:
tea, cake, sandwich, and tea again.

48
00:02:36,074 --> 00:02:38,720
A list can hold the same value twice.

49
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:40,942
Line 3 prints the whole list.

50
00:02:40,962 --> 00:02:43,517
Line 4 prints the item at index 0.

51
00:02:43,567 --> 00:02:47,817
Lines 5 and 6 print the items at index 2
and minus 1.

52
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:50,740
And line 7 uses len, from Chapter 3.

53
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:53,313
For a list, it counts the items.

54
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:09,810
Line 5 prints sandwich, the third item,
because counting starts at 0.

55
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:12,956
Line 6 prints tea, the last item.

56
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:13,981
Step 2.

57
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:18,559
A method is a function that belongs to a
value, like upper for strings.

58
00:03:18,579 --> 00:03:21,590
Lists have methods that change the list
itself.

59
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:23,763
Append adds an item at the end.

60
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,810
Insert puts an item at an index, and moves
the rest along.

61
00:03:27,833 --> 00:03:31,403
An index with an equals sign replaces one
item.

62
00:03:31,433 --> 00:03:34,483
Remove deletes the first item that
matches.

63
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:38,040
And pop removes the last item, and gives
it back to you.

64
00:03:38,653 --> 00:03:41,314
Asha's order starts with tea and cake.

65
00:03:41,334 --> 00:03:43,923
Line 3 appends a sandwich at the end.

66
00:03:43,967 --> 00:03:47,638
Line 5 inserts juice at index 0, at the
front.

67
00:03:47,658 --> 00:03:50,447
Everything else moves along by one.

68
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:55,105
Line 7 replaces the item at index 1 with
green tea.

69
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:06,814
Tea.

70
00:04:06,834 --> 00:04:12,040
After line 5, juice is at index 0, so tea
moved to index 1.

71
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:14,177
Line 9 removes cake.

72
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:19,662
And line 11 pops the last item, sandwich,
and keeps it in the variable last.

73
00:04:19,682 --> 00:04:22,619
Line 12 prints it, then the shorter list.

74
00:04:23,217 --> 00:04:23,612
Step 3.

75
00:04:23,632 --> 00:04:26,994
A slice takes a part of a list, and gives
you a new list.

76
00:04:27,014 --> 00:04:30,317
You write a start and a stop, with a colon
between them.

77
00:04:30,367 --> 00:04:34,495
It starts at the start index, and stops
before the stop index.

78
00:04:34,515 --> 00:04:36,637
Exactly like range in Chapter 8.

79
00:04:36,667 --> 00:04:39,321
Leave out the start, and it starts at 0.

80
00:04:39,341 --> 00:04:42,197
Leave out the stop, and it goes to the
end.

81
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:45,156
And a slice never goes past the end of the
list.

82
00:04:45,176 --> 00:04:46,995
It stops there, with no error.

83
00:04:47,587 --> 00:04:49,343
Here are five sales.

84
00:04:49,363 --> 00:04:51,653
Line 3 slices from 1 to 3.

85
00:05:03,013 --> 00:05:06,410
8.0 and 15.75: the items at index 1 and 2.

86
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:09,583
Index 3 is the stop, so it is left out.

87
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:13,419
Line 4 has no start, so it gives the first
two.

88
00:05:13,439 --> 00:05:17,873
Line 5 has no stop, so it goes from index
2 to the end.

89
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:22,180
Line 6 starts at minus 2, so it gives the
last two sales.

90
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,210
And line 7 asks for items up to index 10.

91
00:05:25,230 --> 00:05:29,202
The list has only five, so the slice stops
at the end.

92
00:05:29,222 --> 00:05:29,867
No error.

93
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:30,887
Step 4.

94
00:05:30,907 --> 00:05:33,560
Here is the fix for the game that forgot.

95
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:37,930
Line 2 makes an empty list: two brackets
with nothing between them.

96
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:39,871
The loop asks three times.

97
00:05:39,891 --> 00:05:45,127
Line 5 appends each guess, so the list
grows by one item on every pass.

98
00:05:45,167 --> 00:05:50,134
Line 7 goes through the list with for, one
item at a time, as it went through a

99
00:05:50,154 --> 00:05:51,397
string in Chapter 8.

100
00:05:51,433 --> 00:05:54,943
And the word in checks whether a value is
in the list.

101
00:05:54,963 --> 00:05:56,446
It gives True or False.

102
00:06:07,813 --> 00:06:08,181
False.

103
00:06:08,201 --> 00:06:10,053
20 is not one of the guesses.

104
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:12,808
And the game remembers every guess.

105
00:06:12,828 --> 00:06:14,600
Line 6 shows all three.

106
00:06:14,633 --> 00:06:18,779
12 is in the list, so line 10 prints, You
tried 12.

107
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:19,778
Step 5.

108
00:06:19,798 --> 00:06:23,750
There are two ways to sort a list, and the
difference matters.

109
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,923
The function sorted makes a new sorted
list.

110
00:06:26,943 --> 00:06:29,280
The original list stays the same.

111
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,940
The method sort changes the list itself.

112
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:35,929
And it gives back None, a special value
that means no value.

113
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:38,654
Line 3 prints sorted sales.

114
00:06:38,674 --> 00:06:40,809
Line 4 prints sales itself.

115
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,634
The original order: 12.5, 8.0, 15.75,
9.25.

116
00:06:55,654 --> 00:06:59,270
Sorted made a new list, and left sales
alone.

117
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:04,140
Line 5 uses sort, so now the list itself
is sorted, smallest first.

118
00:07:04,167 --> 00:07:09,887
Reverse equals True sorts from the biggest
to the smallest.

119
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:15,499
And sum, min and max give the total, the
smallest and the biggest number.

120
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:16,607
Breather.

121
00:07:16,627 --> 00:07:18,863
You now have every piece of the mission.

122
00:07:18,883 --> 00:07:20,216
Let's put them together.

123
00:07:24,950 --> 00:07:26,605
Back to the mission.

124
00:07:26,625 --> 00:07:28,615
Line 2 keeps five sales.

125
00:07:28,635 --> 00:07:30,960
Line 3 appends a sixth, 6.5.

126
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,051
Line 4 adds them up with sum.

127
00:07:33,071 --> 00:07:35,050
Line 5 counts them with len.

128
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:39,891
Line 7 divides the total by len, so the
average stays right when more sales

129
00:07:39,911 --> 00:07:40,340
arrive.

130
00:07:40,367 --> 00:07:45,295
Lines 8 and 9 use max and min, and line 10
slices the last three.

131
00:07:45,315 --> 00:07:51,157
Line 11 makes a sorted copy, biggest
first, and line 12 slices its first two.

132
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:54,514
And line 13 prints the list in the order
of the day.

133
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:08,990
21.0 and 15.75, the two biggest sales.

134
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:16,207
And because line 11 used sorted, not sort,
the last line still shows the day in

135
00:08:16,227 --> 00:08:16,753
order.

136
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,069
Mission complete!

137
00:08:18,089 --> 00:08:21,100
Next, Asha wants a top three sales line.

138
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:25,345
She builds it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

139
00:08:25,917 --> 00:08:29,256
This chapter's AI skill is the worked
example.

140
00:08:29,276 --> 00:08:35,245
When AI explains a slice or a method, ask
for a small list, the code and the exact

141
00:08:35,265 --> 00:08:35,757
result.

142
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:38,006
Then run the example yourself.

143
00:08:38,026 --> 00:08:41,643
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

144
00:08:42,253 --> 00:08:43,189
Stage 1: plan.

145
00:08:43,209 --> 00:08:48,515
Asha wants the three biggest sales,
biggest first, and the day order must stay

146
00:08:48,535 --> 00:08:49,130
the same.

147
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:51,383
She asks for steps, with no code.

148
00:08:51,433 --> 00:08:54,647
The AI's plan: a sorted copy, biggest
first.

149
00:08:54,667 --> 00:08:56,632
A slice of its first three.

150
00:08:56,652 --> 00:08:57,440
Print them.

151
00:08:57,460 --> 00:09:00,453
Then print sales, to check the day order.

152
00:09:01,053 --> 00:09:02,128
Stage 2: write.

153
00:09:02,148 --> 00:09:08,039
Asha writes a slice herself, and asks the
AI to explain it with her own list as a

154
00:09:08,059 --> 00:09:09,133
worked example.

155
00:09:09,167 --> 00:09:13,345
The AI says: the first three items, in the
order of the list.

156
00:09:13,365 --> 00:09:14,447
Not the biggest.

157
00:09:14,467 --> 00:09:15,405
She runs it.

158
00:09:15,425 --> 00:09:17,640
The example matches the run.

159
00:09:18,253 --> 00:09:19,702
Stage 3: generate.

160
00:09:19,722 --> 00:09:23,373
Asha asks for the code, and the exact
output.

161
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:26,904
The AI promises 21.0, 15.75 and 12.5.

162
00:09:26,924 --> 00:09:29,380
It sounds completely sure.

163
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:30,950
Look at line 3.

164
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:35,242
Remember which way sort puts the numbers.

165
00:09:46,613 --> 00:09:46,791
No.

166
00:09:46,811 --> 00:09:51,938
Sort puts the smallest first, so the first
three items are the three smallest.

167
00:09:52,553 --> 00:09:54,046
Asha runs it.

168
00:09:54,066 --> 00:10:00,443
6.5, 8.0 and 9.25: the three smallest
sales of the day.

169
00:10:01,053 --> 00:10:02,167
Stage 4: test.

170
00:10:02,187 --> 00:10:08,319
Asha shows the AI a failed check: the
first of the top three must equal max,

171
00:10:08,339 --> 00:10:08,724
21.0.

172
00:10:08,744 --> 00:10:13,823
She asks for a fix, and three test lists
with expected outputs.

173
00:10:13,867 --> 00:10:15,991
The fix: reverse equals True.

174
00:10:16,011 --> 00:10:17,988
And three expected results.

175
00:10:18,008 --> 00:10:22,574
For a day with only two sales, the AI
predicts an Index Error.

176
00:10:33,947 --> 00:10:34,140
No.

177
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:38,473
Remember step 3: a slice never goes past
the end of the list.

178
00:10:38,493 --> 00:10:40,604
It stops there, with no error.

179
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:43,422
Asha runs all three lists.

180
00:10:43,442 --> 00:10:47,183
A normal day: 21.0 first, and it equals
max.

181
00:10:47,203 --> 00:10:47,610
Pass.

182
00:10:47,633 --> 00:10:51,349
The top three all equal: three fives.

183
00:10:51,369 --> 00:10:51,853
Pass.

184
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:56,611
And only two sales: 9.0 and 4.0, with no
error.

185
00:10:57,220 --> 00:11:01,050
But remember the plan: the day order must
stay the same.

186
00:11:01,070 --> 00:11:03,250
Asha prints sales after the fix.

187
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:04,831
The top three are right.

188
00:11:04,851 --> 00:11:08,643
But sort changed the list itself, so the
day order is gone.

189
00:11:09,253 --> 00:11:10,665
Three test lists pass.

190
00:11:10,685 --> 00:11:14,830
For two sales, the AI predicted an error,
but the run gave none.

191
00:11:14,850 --> 00:11:16,587
A prediction is not a test.

192
00:11:16,607 --> 00:11:17,303
The run is.

193
00:11:17,333 --> 00:11:20,058
And the day order check fails.

194
00:11:20,078 --> 00:11:22,253
Asha must fix that next.

195
00:11:22,853 --> 00:11:23,905
Stage 5: debug.

196
00:11:23,925 --> 00:11:29,408
To keep the day order, Asha stores the
result of sort in a new variable, top.

197
00:11:29,428 --> 00:11:31,623
Python stops with a Type Error.

198
00:11:31,667 --> 00:11:36,429
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

199
00:11:36,449 --> 00:11:39,341
The AI names line 3: sort gives back None.

200
00:11:39,361 --> 00:11:43,569
Not subscriptable means you cannot use
square brackets on it.

201
00:11:43,589 --> 00:11:44,817
The fix is sorted.

202
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:47,491
Asha uses sorted on line 3.

203
00:11:47,511 --> 00:11:49,350
The top three are right.

204
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:52,631
And the day order is still there, on the
last line.

205
00:11:53,253 --> 00:11:55,958
A fix can break a case that worked before.

206
00:11:55,978 --> 00:12:01,149
So Asha runs all three test lists again,
with sorted, and prints each list after

207
00:12:01,169 --> 00:12:01,343
it.

208
00:12:01,367 --> 00:12:05,503
Every top three is right, and every list
keeps its order.

209
00:12:05,523 --> 00:12:07,035
All four checks pass.

210
00:12:07,653 --> 00:12:08,891
Stage 6: improve.

211
00:12:08,911 --> 00:12:13,623
Asha asks for one change that makes the
line shorter or clearer.

212
00:12:13,643 --> 00:12:16,803
And she adds: do not change what it
prints.

213
00:12:16,833 --> 00:12:22,315
The AI suggests: sort normally, and take
the last three items with a negative

214
00:12:22,335 --> 00:12:22,744
slice.

215
00:12:22,764 --> 00:12:24,959
It says the output is the same.

216
00:12:25,553 --> 00:12:26,667
Asha tests it.

217
00:12:26,687 --> 00:12:28,934
Line 3 sorts smallest first.

218
00:12:28,954 --> 00:12:31,200
Line 4 takes the last three.

219
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:42,782
No.

220
00:12:42,802 --> 00:12:48,193
The same three sales, but in the opposite
order: the biggest is now last.

221
00:12:48,213 --> 00:12:50,490
The AI ignored her instruction.

222
00:12:50,533 --> 00:12:52,979
A different order is a different output.

223
00:12:52,999 --> 00:12:54,828
Asha keeps her sorted version.

224
00:12:55,420 --> 00:12:56,282
Quick check.

225
00:12:56,302 --> 00:12:58,709
An AI explains what a slice does.

226
00:12:58,729 --> 00:13:00,179
What do you do next?

227
00:13:11,547 --> 00:13:11,676
D.

228
00:13:11,696 --> 00:13:15,190
Run its worked example, and compare the
result.

229
00:13:15,787 --> 00:13:16,389
Breather.

230
00:13:16,409 --> 00:13:21,646
Asha caught three AI mistakes: the three
smallest sales, an error that never

231
00:13:21,666 --> 00:13:23,790
happens, and a change of order.

232
00:13:23,810 --> 00:13:26,903
Next, the mistakes beginners make with
lists.

233
00:13:31,650 --> 00:13:32,494
Mistake 1.

234
00:13:32,514 --> 00:13:36,985
This list has four items, so its indexes
are 0 to 3.

235
00:13:37,005 --> 00:13:38,540
Line 3 prints tea.

236
00:13:38,567 --> 00:13:42,072
Line 4 asks for index 4, which does not
exist.

237
00:13:42,092 --> 00:13:46,517
Python stops with an Index Error: list
index out of range.

238
00:13:46,567 --> 00:13:50,496
The fix: the last index is the length
minus 1.

239
00:13:50,516 --> 00:13:51,784
Or use minus 1.

240
00:13:52,387 --> 00:13:53,277
Now you try.

241
00:13:53,297 --> 00:13:57,828
Line 3 sorts the sales, and stores the
result back in sales.

242
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:09,487
None.

243
00:14:09,507 --> 00:14:13,810
Sort gives back None, so line 3 replaced
the whole list with None.

244
00:14:13,833 --> 00:14:18,678
The fix: write sales dot sort on its own
line, or use sorted.

245
00:14:19,287 --> 00:14:20,064
Mistake 3.

246
00:14:20,084 --> 00:14:21,897
The list has tea twice.

247
00:14:21,917 --> 00:14:24,607
Line 3 removes only the first tea.

248
00:14:24,633 --> 00:14:28,058
Line 5 removes juice, which is not in the
list.

249
00:14:28,078 --> 00:14:30,403
Python stops with a Value Error.

250
00:14:30,433 --> 00:14:34,536
The fix: check with in first, and remove
only when it is there.

251
00:14:35,153 --> 00:14:35,809
Mistake 4.

252
00:14:35,829 --> 00:14:38,579
Line 2 has no comma between tea and cake.

253
00:14:38,599 --> 00:14:43,713
Python joins two strings that stand next
to each other, so you get one item:

254
00:14:43,733 --> 00:14:44,253
teacake.

255
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:48,453
There is no error, but the list has 2
items, not 3.

256
00:14:48,473 --> 00:14:50,499
Check the count with len.

257
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:51,964
Quick check.

258
00:14:51,984 --> 00:14:53,331
A list has 5 items.

259
00:14:53,351 --> 00:14:57,649
What is the index of the last item,
counting from the start?

260
00:15:09,013 --> 00:15:09,189
A.

261
00:15:09,209 --> 00:15:09,384
4.

262
00:15:09,404 --> 00:15:14,070
Counting starts at 0, so the indexes are 0
to 4.

263
00:15:14,687 --> 00:15:15,547
Last breather.

264
00:15:15,567 --> 00:15:19,446
Now you know the four list mistakes that
catch most beginners.

265
00:15:24,183 --> 00:15:24,581
First.

266
00:15:24,601 --> 00:15:26,184
A player types 7 twice.

267
00:15:26,204 --> 00:15:31,760
How could the word in let the game say,
you already guessed 7, and not count the

268
00:15:31,780 --> 00:15:32,526
second try?

269
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:46,420
Try it in your editor after the video.

270
00:15:47,020 --> 00:15:47,507
Second.

271
00:15:47,527 --> 00:15:48,737
Range has a step.

272
00:15:48,757 --> 00:15:51,994
A slice can have one too: two colons, then
2.

273
00:15:52,014 --> 00:15:53,803
Which sales does it give?

274
00:15:53,823 --> 00:15:55,757
Predict first, then run it.

275
00:16:07,113 --> 00:16:09,760
Compare your prediction with the real run.

276
00:16:10,350 --> 00:16:11,188
Let's recap.

277
00:16:11,208 --> 00:16:13,618
A list keeps many values in order.

278
00:16:13,638 --> 00:16:17,120
Indexes start at 0, and minus 1 is the
last item.

279
00:16:17,167 --> 00:16:20,047
A list can change: append, insert, remove
and pop.

280
00:16:20,067 --> 00:16:24,947
A slice stops before its stop index, and
never goes past the end.

281
00:16:24,967 --> 00:16:28,587
For goes through a list, and in checks
whether a value is in it.

282
00:16:28,633 --> 00:16:30,185
Sorted gives a new list.

283
00:16:30,205 --> 00:16:32,673
Sort changes the list, and gives None.

284
00:16:32,700 --> 00:16:36,790
And with AI: ask for a worked example,
then run it yourself.

285
00:16:37,387 --> 00:16:38,665
One last check.

286
00:16:38,685 --> 00:16:40,656
Sales holds 3, 9 and 1.

287
00:16:40,676 --> 00:16:44,466
What does sorted sales, index minus 1,
give?

288
00:16:55,847 --> 00:16:56,030
B.

289
00:16:56,050 --> 00:16:56,233
9.

290
00:16:56,253 --> 00:17:01,416
Sorted gives 1, 3, 9, and minus 1 is the
last item.

291
00:17:02,020 --> 00:17:03,301
Mission complete!

292
00:17:03,321 --> 00:17:06,746
You earned 100 XP and the List Builder
badge.

293
00:17:08,983 --> 00:17:10,560
Now it is your turn.

294
00:17:10,580 --> 00:17:14,393
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

295
00:17:14,433 --> 00:17:17,743
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

296
00:17:17,767 --> 00:17:21,647
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a shopping list.

297
00:17:21,667 --> 00:17:27,237
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each: a
game that remembers, and an AI's top

298
00:17:27,257 --> 00:17:28,267
scores to fix.

299
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:29,902
Predict first, then run.

300
00:17:29,922 --> 00:17:32,808
Try each task before you open the
solution.

301
00:17:33,383 --> 00:17:35,806
Lists find a value by its position.

302
00:17:35,826 --> 00:17:37,830
But what is the price of tea?

303
00:17:37,850 --> 00:17:39,853
A list cannot answer by name.

304
00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:42,653
In Chapter 12, you will learn
dictionaries.

305
00:17:42,673 --> 00:17:46,070
They find a value by its name, like the
price of tea.

306
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:47,755
Do the warm-up tasks now.

307
00:17:47,775 --> 00:17:49,630
They take about ten minutes.

308
00:17:49,667 --> 00:17:51,397
See you in Chapter 12.
